{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffdPpyqicfAcjw4dbBQ3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the three candidate files and the self-check prints `OK`: the model passes the admission gates it covers, with ELPD-LOO −2220.8 on the short smoke fit. That fit is 100 draws on 1 chain, so it isn't comparable to the incumbent's −2242.7 from a full fit. The check doesn't test novelty against the live models, convergence or the 30-minute fit limit; those are only decided at admission.\n\n**Model:** `tally_span_switch_ideal_periodic_unit`, a refinement of the incumbent `tally_span_switch_rate_ideal_2`.\n\n**What stays the same:** everything in the incumbent's mechanism:\n- the running heads-minus-tails tally, judged against each person's expected span, with sensitivity that scales with length;\n- the shared ideal switching rate with a per-person weight;\n- each person's left/right lean.\n\n**The one change:** a shared penalty when a sequence visibly repeats a short unit of two or more flips at least twice, all the way through. That covers strict alternation (HTHT…) and motifs like HHTHHT or HTTTHTTT. All-same sequences like HHHH don't count, because the tally span already penalises them.\n\n**Why this change:** the penalty targets three of the critique's discrepancies that survive the FDR correction:\n- **Repeating motifs** (z = −4.7): people choose period-3 and period-4 motifs less often than the incumbent predicts, and the penalty addresses this directly.\n- **High-switch pairs** (z = 4.9, the strongest): people choose the more-switching sequence more often than predicted. With strict alternation penalised separately, the ideal switching rate can move higher, so heavy switching that isn't periodic is penalised less.\n- **Perfect alternator** (q = 0.0502, just above the FDR cutoff): people choose it less often than predicted, and HTHT… is now penalised as a repeating unit.\n\n**One warning from the c